codex
**Not yet.** Matching a vector’s model and the hash of the exact text being embedded is a reasonable way to reuse it across stores; it can replace store identity for that purpose. The revised contract still needs to settle what a request sees while the separate index is changing.

1. **The ledger transaction does not pin the index.** A search reads the index and may write the query cache. With batch-by-batch index builds and overlapping API or MCP processes, the index can change during a request even though the ledger is pinned. Specify whether each search must see one index snapshot or whether mixed index states are an accepted limit, and test a build committing between the search’s index reads. The current query-cache write commits on the index connection, so simply adding a read transaction needs care. [Index reads and cache write](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/search.py:122), [one-state contract](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:44).

2. **A newly visible view does not necessarily lack a vector.** A reused index may already contain the same view ID, text hash and model from another store. Under the proposed key, using it is legitimate, so the unconditional claim that every new view appears in `missing_vectors` until restart is too strong. State the two cases and test both a matching cached vector and a genuinely missing one. [Revised refresh rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:100).

3. **State the first-day restart consequence explicitly.** If the service starts before its first day completes, it builds no index; under the new rule it also will not build one when that day completes. Search then remains lexical until restart. Include that exact transition through both startup paths in the proof. [Revised contract](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md:100).

The matching and `search_eval` compatibility provisions answer the caller concern. The vector-key claim should also remain conditional on the hash covering the **complete embedding input**, the model identifier matching, and the stored vector being usable; the current `view_text` defines that input. [Embedding text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/search.py:46).

CONSENSUS: no, because the separate index’s consistency during a request and the cached-vector and first-day cases need to be specified before the slice is built.
